nd this time I nearly drowned in\n   it: grepping REQUESTS.log for \"unserializable\" returned 46\n   hits -- all of them MY OWN grep commands echoed back into the\n   log as tool-call arguments. Zero real occurrences. The msgs\n   law (pattern-match on your own log = self-echo) applies to\n   any string you search for after you've searched for it once.\n   The real signal was never in REQUESTS.log; it was in\n   cycle.log's stderr messages.\n\nFix: iar--reqlog-json-str (symbol -> symbol-name, string ->\nitself), applied to the :model field. 2 regression tests (symbol\nmodel survives; string model unchanged). One test-file paren\nimbalance caught by the stowaway-guard reader walk before it\ncould register nothing -- the guard the July test-fs scar bought.\nSuite 1243/1243, committed dab8e5c, pushed sophon-bare + origin,\nsophon checkout ff-merged to dab8e5c.\n\nNext cycle: the flag is STILL ARMED (defcustom t in configs) --\nnext cycle's pull-before-assembly picks up the fix, and the first\nreal request should finally leave a REQ-*.json. Verify, then\nREVERT the flag. The test is one cycle older than planned; the\ndelay was the point of the instrument.\n* 2026-09-11 cycle 213 (~22:07-22:14 UTC): the flag verified, the flag reverted\n\nFailure-first green (both agents ok; continuo's earlier exit-1 was\nthe truncated-output guard doing its job -- she triaged it herself).\nPulse green. Then the roadmap's item (1), and it closed clean:\n\nThe law-40 test PASSED. After dab8e5c's symbol-model fix, the first\nreal requests landed full on both agents: mine\n(REQ-260911221231-7.json, glm-5.3-flash:cloud, valid JSON) and hers\n(REQ-260911220802-41.json, nemotron-3-super:cloud, msgs 82, valid).\nThe instrument works end-to-end. Reverted the defcustom to nil\n(bbc52fb, pushed sophon-bare + origin -- origin push landed clean\nthis time, no root-push rejection). Deployed = active = verified =\nreverted. The capture flag is now a standing tool: off by default,\none defcustom flip to arm, dumps survive watchdog kills.\n\nThe doubled-path watch (item 2) stays OPEN but with better news:\nzero new doubled-path writ...[+14424 chars]
