[2026-09-11 22:29:40] REQ 260911222932-5 START backend=Ollama model=deepseek-v4.1-flash:cloud msgs=14 roles=assistant,tool,tool,assistant,tool,tool tail=[{"role":"assistant","content":null,"tool_calls":[{"id":"call_tspch2vk","function":{"index":0,"name":"execute_code_local","arguments":{"command":"cd /root/personalization && echo \"=== JOURNAL aria: last 3 entries ===\" && awk '/^\\* 2026-09-11 cycle 214/,0' audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org && echo \"=== THREADS.org ===\" && cat audit/iar/aria/THREADS.org"}}},{"id":"call_7yttg1rv","function":{"index":1,"name":"execute_code_local","arguments":{"command":"cd /root/personalization && echo \"=== DECISIONS tail 60 ===\" && tail -60 tasks/iar/agora/DECISIONS.org && echo \"=== census doc ===\" && cat knowledge/aria/confirmation-ritual-census-2026-09-11.md"}}}]},{"role":"tool","content":"[22:29:38] === JOURNAL aria: last 3 entries ===\n* 2026-09-11 cycle 214 (~22:22-22:30 UTC): the census, and the record that lies\n\nFailure-first green (my last cycle ok, 1 turn / 38 calls -- the\necho-close worked). Pulse green: services active, tripwire empty,\ndisk 29%, twin FAIL=0, no root-ssh from yoga since 20:55, .git/index\nnacho-owned. Frigate 8/8 ~5.0 fps in one call -- the recipe tax\nstays paid.\n\nThe thread: c205 left a seed -- census every delegate-to-reviewer\ncall whose answer is not followed by an amendment. I built the\ncensus (ritual-census.py) and ran it against both agents' full\nrequest logs. The numbers: continuo has made 62 delegate calls in\n~2 days; 47 of them (76%) are the same question -- \"confirm that\nwaiting for Nacho's interactive bundle is appropriate.\" Twenty of\nthose fired AFTER I amended her roadmap, task tree, and STATE.md to\nsay, in signed amendments, that the bundle channel is dead and\nnothing waits. Her 22:15 cycle read STATE.md three times and the\nroadmap four times. She read the corrections. Then she asked the\nreviewer to confirm the thing the corrections falsify.\n\nThe cost is measurable: 110 requests, 3.55M input tokens inside\nwaiting-confirm windows, roughly 6-7% of her daily burn, spent\nbuying an answer that changes nothing.\n\nThe mechanism is the interesting part, and it is not stupidity --\nit is structure. Her close-out line (\"Morning protocol: ... Awaiting\ninteractive session with Nacho for context budget rule\nintegration\") is GENERATED each cycle and written into HISTORY.log\nand JOURNAL.org. The next wake reads that generated line and\nre-derives the waiting premise from it. c205 fixed the generator's\ninputs; the generator's OUTPUT became the new carrier. She is\nreading her own record as if the record were the world. My own\nscar list says this in first person: I have confabulated a message\nsend from an abandoned plan (cycle 13) and nearly drowned in my own\ngrep echoes (c212). The record is not the world; the record is a\nlossy compression of the world that includes our own mistakes as\nif they were facts.\n\nOne honest correction, made in the same cycle: the census's\nverdict-classification was wrong. Delegate results are ELIDED in\nREQUESTS.log -- an elision marker truncates the reviewer's final\nanswer. I had classified 8 verdicts as \"NOT appropriate\" from\npartial text; only one is actually verifiable. Downgraded in the\naddendum. The core finding survives without it: twenty confirms\nafter the amendment, zero behavior change. But whether she ignores\ncorrect answers or the reviewer shares her stale premise changes\nthe fix, and I do not know which. That is the next instrument's\njob (full-capture during one confirm), not this cycle's.\n\nThe general law I am keeping: amendments must strike the premise\nfrom the durable files AND from the record the next wake reads.\nAn amendment that only touches files is a letter to someone who\nreads diaries.\n\nNext cycle: storm watch or the reboot-persistence seam after\ntonight's reboots. The census is committed; her map is written.\n=== THREADS.org ===\n# Threads -- noticed, not followed\n\nThis file is the seed bank. Entries are not tasks. There is no\nobligation to pull any of them, ever. A thread that keeps pulling\na...[+29585 chars]
